WEBVTT

00:00:00.119 --> 00:00:04.179
What could you build with a neural network whose architecture came from an

00:00:04.319 --> 00:00:04.719
animal?

00:00:05.189 --> 00:00:09.610
A connectome gives you that starting point: a map of an animal's

00:00:09.729 --> 00:00:12.169
neurons and the connections between them.

00:00:13.169 --> 00:00:17.750
At the sports betting company bet three sixty-five, we're exploring

00:00:17.809 --> 00:00:21.829
how circuitry shaped by an animal's life responds to a

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:24.249
completely different kind of information.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:29.030
Sports gives us a familiar problem to bring to that unfamiliar

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:29.829
architecture.

00:00:30.329 --> 00:00:34.889
A biological neuron is a cell with branches that reach toward other

00:00:34.969 --> 00:00:39.530
cells. Where they make contact, a junction called a synapse

00:00:39.589 --> 00:00:44.049
carries signals between them. Researchers trace those cells and

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:47.150
contacts through real tissue to build the map.

00:00:47.679 --> 00:00:52.379
Connectomes come in different sizes: some cover a single circuit,

00:00:52.679 --> 00:00:54.999
some a region, some a whole brain.

00:00:55.619 --> 00:01:00.240
The one behind this project covers a male fruit fly's brain together with

00:01:00.259 --> 00:01:04.899
its ventral nerve cord, the bundle of nervous tissue running down into

00:01:04.919 --> 00:01:08.919
its body — roughly a hundred and sixty-seven thousand

00:01:08.959 --> 00:01:12.659
neurons, in a volume smaller than a sesame seed.

00:01:13.169 --> 00:01:16.449
Recovering a map like that is slow physical work.

00:01:17.089 --> 00:01:21.510
The tissue is preserved and sliced far thinner than a wavelength of light,

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:25.430
and every slice goes under an electron microscope.

00:01:26.089 --> 00:01:30.709
What comes back is a stack of tens of thousands of images in which

00:01:30.769 --> 00:01:35.150
a neuron appears only as a cross-section, a smudge that

00:01:35.269 --> 00:01:39.810
has to be matched to the smudge above it and the smudge below it, through

00:01:39.909 --> 00:01:44.050
every branch and every turn, past thousands of

00:01:44.149 --> 00:01:46.629
neighboring cells doing the same thing.

00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:51.019
Google Research built the machine-learning systems that draft those

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:55.720
tracings, working with HHMI Janelia, the University

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:59.739
of Cambridge, and the MRC Laboratory of Molecular

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:03.879
Biology, whose scientists proofread and corrected the result.

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:09.100
The imaging and tracing methods behind it were developed over decades.

00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:13.319
This particular resource went public in June twenty

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.699
twenty-six, and the paper followed in September.

00:02:17.209 --> 00:02:20.689
What an engineer downloads at the end of all that is a graph.

00:02:21.329 --> 00:02:25.550
Neurons are the nodes, connections are the edges, and each edge

00:02:25.649 --> 00:02:29.669
carries the number of synaptic contacts the two cells make with each

00:02:29.729 --> 00:02:33.409
other. The graph arrives with structure already in it:

00:02:33.969 --> 00:02:38.669
dedicated routes for vision, for smell, for driving legs

00:02:38.729 --> 00:02:43.350
and wings, for holding onto something that happened a moment ago.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:47.369
Nobody drew that layout on a whiteboard.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:50.689
It is there because it kept flies alive.

00:02:51.199 --> 00:02:55.659
To make that graph respond, we give its neurons equations that describe

00:02:55.699 --> 00:02:59.900
their behavior: how much voltage builds in a modeled neuron as

00:03:00.019 --> 00:03:04.559
inputs arrive, how much charge crosses a synapse, when the cell

00:03:04.619 --> 00:03:07.459
fires, and how quickly it settles afterward.

00:03:07.939 --> 00:03:12.519
Those equations come out of the experimental literature — fly neurons

00:03:12.559 --> 00:03:17.199
have been recorded from for decades, and published spiking models of fly

00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:19.459
circuits are already there to work from.

00:03:20.359 --> 00:03:24.119
So the wiring comes out of the animal, the timing comes out of a

00:03:24.239 --> 00:03:27.739
simulator, and running the thing means running both.

00:03:28.229 --> 00:03:30.169
Which brings us to the awkward part.

00:03:30.669 --> 00:03:34.149
A fruit fly's nervous system has no input for baseball.

00:03:34.829 --> 00:03:39.510
It has inputs for light, for odor molecules, for the taste of something

00:03:39.709 --> 00:03:44.390
sweet and the taste of something bitter, for the position of its own legs.

00:03:45.189 --> 00:03:49.669
We named our project bet36fly after the animal

00:03:49.709 --> 00:03:53.729
supplying the wiring. Our first engineering problem is the

00:03:53.909 --> 00:03:54.470
adapter.

00:03:54.979 --> 00:03:56.679
We use the taste pathways.

00:03:57.239 --> 00:04:01.599
The pregame numbers for a team — recent form, the starting pitcher,

00:04:02.099 --> 00:04:06.619
the ordinary inputs any baseball model takes — become patterns of

00:04:06.699 --> 00:04:10.679
stimulation on the food-attraction and food-avoidance pathways,

00:04:11.339 --> 00:04:15.619
an engineered version of putting something sweet or something bitter in

00:04:15.659 --> 00:04:16.599
front of the animal.

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:21.709
Each team's stimulus uses only information available before the game.

00:04:22.569 --> 00:04:26.529
Both teams follow the same encoding rules and are presented

00:04:26.569 --> 00:04:31.229
separately. Both can taste good; the encoding preserves

00:04:31.269 --> 00:04:32.970
their individual qualities.

00:04:33.499 --> 00:04:35.120
Then you run it and watch.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.340
Activity spreads from the taste inputs through whatever the wiring allows,

00:04:40.799 --> 00:04:44.959
settles, and leaves a pattern across a set of neurons we chose in

00:04:44.979 --> 00:04:49.359
advance. That pattern is what we read out, and a small

00:04:49.459 --> 00:04:53.679
external model, trained on past seasons, turns the two

00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:55.419
patterns into a prediction.

00:04:55.889 --> 00:05:00.329
If you work with embeddings, the shape will be familiar: something

00:05:00.369 --> 00:05:04.929
goes in, a representation comes out of the middle, and an output layer

00:05:05.089 --> 00:05:09.770
reads the representation. We designed the input adapter and the

00:05:09.909 --> 00:05:14.070
readout. The connections inside the circuit come from the measured

00:05:14.109 --> 00:05:18.469
anatomy, and the simulator follows activity through those connections

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:19.269
over time.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:24.199
Sports gives us a way to test this chain against outcomes we can check.

00:05:24.859 --> 00:05:29.219
For our first full-season test, we fixed the model before giving it the

00:05:29.299 --> 00:05:32.539
twenty twenty-three Major League Baseball games.

00:05:32.719 --> 00:05:37.040
Across more than twenty-four hundred games, it called about fifty-six

00:05:37.139 --> 00:05:41.600
percent correctly, level with a conventional model given the same

00:05:41.739 --> 00:05:46.360
pregame information. We are still investigating which features of the

00:05:46.499 --> 00:05:49.799
fly's wiring contribute useful capabilities.

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:54.969
The circuit gives that investigation a physical reference: named

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:58.509
cells and the connections measured between them.

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:02.449
One experiment we can design is to quiet a selected pathway,

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:06.689
present the same matchup again, and compare the response.

00:06:07.239 --> 00:06:11.859
Comparing the intact circuit with altered wiring is how we can test

00:06:11.999 --> 00:06:15.360
which parts of the anatomy contribute to the representation.

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:18.939
We can also let experience change the connections.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:23.499
A living fly carries the previous encounter into the next one.

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:28.580
A fly that finds food while a particular smell is in the air will

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:32.399
treat that smell differently the next time it drifts past.

00:06:32.939 --> 00:06:37.439
The structure behind that is called the mushroom body, where sensory

00:06:37.599 --> 00:06:40.779
signals meet a teaching signal carried by dopamine.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:45.840
We implemented an association experiment in a separate version of our

00:06:45.979 --> 00:06:50.199
simulator: pair a cue with the teaching signal, wait,

00:06:50.539 --> 00:06:54.660
then present the cue on its own, and the circuit answers it differently

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:55.779
than it did before.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:00.879
The change stays specific to the cue that was trained, and it reverses

00:07:00.899 --> 00:07:02.759
when the teaching signal reverses.

00:07:03.599 --> 00:07:08.159
This lets us study a representation that changes with experience,

00:07:08.319 --> 00:07:11.999
alongside the fixed responses used in the season test.

00:07:12.469 --> 00:07:15.749
Once you have an adapter into the circuit, you can give it a different

00:07:15.809 --> 00:07:20.289
environment. A developer connected the same released wiring to Doom,

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:24.589
the first-person shooter, in a project called DOOMFLY.

00:07:25.489 --> 00:07:29.709
Game frames go in as input, the code path runs through the retained

00:07:29.769 --> 00:07:34.129
connectome graph, and a fixed set of outputs is mapped onto moving and

00:07:34.269 --> 00:07:34.669
shooting.

00:07:35.179 --> 00:07:38.199
The screen alone can hide how a demo works.

00:07:38.899 --> 00:07:42.739
A browser can also show a convincing fly animation using

00:07:42.859 --> 00:07:45.739
JavaScript and entirely scripted movement.

00:07:46.859 --> 00:07:51.299
When you watch a fly demo online, look for what controls its next

00:07:51.419 --> 00:07:55.120
action. In DOOMFLY, you can open the

00:07:55.179 --> 00:07:59.440
repository and follow a game frame through the neural model to a

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:00.239
keypress.

00:08:00.789 --> 00:08:04.529
In Doom, each action changes the next game frame.

00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:09.209
Turning alters the view; moving brings something closer.

00:08:09.849 --> 00:08:13.309
The circuit's outputs become part of its next input.

00:08:13.899 --> 00:08:18.140
Giving that loop the sensory detail and physical consequences of an

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:20.879
animal's world is a larger undertaking.

00:08:21.799 --> 00:08:26.439
FlyVis develops the visual side: researchers constrain a model

00:08:26.499 --> 00:08:31.000
with measured fly connectivity, train it on visual tasks, and

00:08:31.019 --> 00:08:34.359
compare its responses with recordings from real cells.

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:39.399
FlyBody supplies a body in a physics engine, with reinforcement-learning

00:08:39.439 --> 00:08:41.459
controllers for walking and flight.

00:08:42.079 --> 00:08:46.220
It provides a way to test the consequences of a controller's outputs,

00:08:46.799 --> 00:08:49.340
down to whether the fly keeps its feet on the ground.

00:08:49.879 --> 00:08:54.280
The reason all of this is arriving at once is that two very different

00:08:54.339 --> 00:08:59.000
timescales met. The imaging, the tracing, the proofreading

00:08:59.459 --> 00:09:03.399
— that is decades of method and years of work on this one specimen,

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:08.339
released in June. The experiments built on top of it are weeks

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:08.739
old.

00:09:09.249 --> 00:09:13.789
Reading into an unfamiliar literature, writing an adapter, getting

00:09:13.909 --> 00:09:18.530
somebody else's research code to run used to be the slow parts,

00:09:18.589 --> 00:09:22.889
and coding agents built on large language models have made them cheap

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:27.650
enough that a small team can cross from neuroscience to a running pipeline

00:09:27.709 --> 00:09:28.389
in days.

00:09:28.869 --> 00:09:30.589
And the maps keep improving.

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:35.270
A larval zebrafish is transparent, which means you can point a

00:09:35.309 --> 00:09:39.770
microscope at a living one and watch neurons light up across most of its

00:09:39.869 --> 00:09:41.849
brain while the animal behaves.

00:09:42.379 --> 00:09:46.760
Google Research is working toward pairing a zebrafish wiring map

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:49.939
with activity recorded from the same specimen.

00:09:50.959 --> 00:09:55.200
The associated activity dataset already covers more than seventy

00:09:55.399 --> 00:09:59.260
thousand neurons. You could run your equations on the measured

00:09:59.339 --> 00:10:03.379
wiring, compare the result with what those same neurons did,

00:10:03.839 --> 00:10:07.539
and tighten the model where the two disagree.

00:10:07.619 --> 00:10:11.919
The recordings would give the simulator a detailed target from the animal

00:10:11.959 --> 00:10:12.899
it represents.

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:16.509
The same approach is reaching mammalian circuits.

00:10:17.029 --> 00:10:21.670
The MICrONS project reconstructed a block of mouse visual cortex,

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:26.309
the brain tissue that processes vision, containing roughly half a

00:10:26.369 --> 00:10:30.489
billion synaptic contacts alongside activity recordings.

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:35.599
Larger mapping programs are underway, bringing increasingly rich circuits

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:37.619
within reach of this kind of experiment.

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:41.759
We started with a network architecture that came from an animal.

00:10:42.559 --> 00:10:47.079
We can now give part of that network an unfamiliar input, measure the

00:10:47.119 --> 00:10:50.920
response, and change a pathway to see what it contributes.

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:54.139
The fly lets us begin doing that work today.

00:10:54.839 --> 00:10:59.439
As maps of fish and mammalian brains develop, engineers will have

00:10:59.459 --> 00:11:04.099
more biological architectures to build around, with recordings that show

00:11:04.139 --> 00:11:06.559
how those circuits worked in the living animal.
